        "title": "Education gender gap",
        "text": "Research into gender differences in education has tended to concentrate on girls' poor performance in science and maths, and has almost ignored boys' low achievement in reading and writing. According to Cecilia Reynolds, of the Ontario Institute for Studies in Education, it has long been recognised that girls did well in literacy and boys did not, but there was no great concern about this, because in the real world boys were still going on to get better jobs and salaries. However, a 2004 study by the Council of Ministers of Education found that Canadian girls are outperforming boys in literacy skills by a wider margin than was previously thought. Among thirteen-year-olds, about ten per cent more girls than boys met the expected targets for literacy, and among sixteen-year-olds, about seventeen per cent more girls write at a level appropriate for their age.\n\nAs if that were not bad enough, recent tests have shown for the first time that the performance gap that once existed between boys and girls in science and maths has now almost disappeared too. Some experts even predict that at some point in the future, girls may actually move ahead of boys in science. According to Paul Cappon, Director General of the Council of Ministers of Education, one result of this will be to put girls in the lead both in university entrance and in achievement in the labour market. Already only forty-two per cent of university graduates in Canada are male, and that figure is falling every year.\n\nExperts agree that the time has come to focus on the long-standing gender divide in literacy, but their explanations for it, and their solutions, vary widely. In a culture that favours equal opportunity and supports political correctness, some have found it hard to discuss this troubling gender gap without entering the sensitive area of sexism.\n\nSome educators have blamed policy, and the way it has been carried out by women teachers, directly: they say that because educational practice has aimed at improving the performance of girls, boys have been forgotten. Cecilia Reynolds has warned that it is important to deal with any differences between boys and girls without putting the blame on anyone. Although she does not believe that boys were forgotten, she does admit that not enough attention may have been paid to their different learning needs.\n\nOthers have pointed to a less obvious cause, which Paul Cappon has called the \"feminisation\" of education. Teaching is increasingly dominated by women, as more young women enter the profession and more older men retire, he says, and this leaves boys with few male role models in the classroom. \"It's women doing the teaching. In the socialisation process, boys will tend to discount the importance of that particular subject area when it's only women teaching it,\" he suggests.\n\nCappon also thinks that the reading materials available in schools may suit girls better than boys. As he points out, the current view is that boys prefer to read factual and instructional material that helps them to understand particular areas of interest, whereas girls are attracted to stories that explore relationships between people.\n\nSince there is now concrete evidence of a significant gender divide, a growing body of opinion argues that the only way to make sure boys and girls are treated fairly is to separate them. Some schools have used this approach to improve girls' performance in science and maths, but with the new emphasis on literacy and boys, some people now say that complete separation is the only solution.\n\nLeonard Sax, the founder of an organisation that promotes single-sex education, points to the success of an inner-city school in Montreal which introduced single-sex classrooms five years ago. Since then, absenteeism has fallen and the proportion of students going on to college has nearly doubled. Sax, a family doctor and psychologist, believes that many people in education are paralysed by a feeling that it is politically incorrect to talk about innate differences in the ways girls and boys learn. He argues, however, that genetic differences between boys and girls make it impossible for both to learn successfully in the same classroom. According to Sax, every important choice made in schools benefits one gender and disadvantages the other. For example, he claims that girls hear better than boys, and that part of the reason why boys fall behind is simply that they cannot hear the teacher. He also points to research showing key differences in the way boys and girls respond to confrontation. Girls shrink away from a confrontational teaching style under which many boys would thrive.\n\nWhat policy makers finally decide to do may depend on how far the results of the recent study are judged to reflect the general situation accurately. Cecilia Reynolds stresses that gender differences are statistical, with significant numbers of individuals everywhere not following the general trend. Although she does not believe we should be seriously concerned, she does think that in future parents and teachers will need to pay more attention to what is happening to boys, both at home and in the classroom."
